artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a low-power wide-area wireless network operating in sub-GHz unlicensed bands using a chirp-spread-spectrum modulation (LoRa-style physical layer), in which end devices transmit to gateways across heterogeneous deployment environments. Let L denote a wireless link between a transmitter at position p_t and a receiver at position p_r, embedded in an environment E that may include open-air regions, line-of-sight (LOS) and non-line-of-sight (NLOS) segments, concrete or steel obstructions, indoor settings (e.g., homes, hospitals, schools), and vegetated areas (e.g., forests). Assume each link experiences a combination of large-scale path loss, small-scale fading, shadowing, and direction-dependent (anisotropic) attenuation due to dynamic and static obstacles that vary with bearing from the transmitter.

Develop a mathematical framework that, given a description of the environment E (e.g., a map of obstacle classes and their electromagnetic properties along the propagation path) and link parameters (carrier frequency, spreading factor, bandwidth, transmit power, antenna heights), provides:

(a) a closed-form or computable model L(p_t, p_r, E) for the expected link quality (e.g., received signal strength, signal-to-noise ratio, and packet reception ratio), explicitly accounting for direction-dependent path loss, multi-medium attenuation, and fading in transitional regions where link quality is temporally unstable;

(b) a coverage-prediction system that, from such a model, outputs the spatial coverage region of a gateway at p_t under environment E, capturing anisotropy and the existence of transitional links;

(c) provable accuracy or error bounds on link-quality estimates and coverage predictions across the listed deployment environments, without requiring extensive on-site measurements.

The problem is to construct such a model and system, or to prove fundamental limits on their achievable accuracy.
